## Correction — the licensing boundary, applied 2026-08-22

This section is appended, not edited over: NOTICE is append-only, so where an
earlier entry above no longer describes what ships, the correction is recorded
here and the earlier entry stays as written.

Michael's ratified boundary: MIT-attributed prose lives only in the artifacts
this repository ships and keeps updating — the skill template files, their
non-rendered `SOURCES.md` / `_sources.md` sidecars, and this file. Anything a
template GENERATES becomes the user's own document and arrives attribution-free
(the CC0 documents-arrive-clean doctrine). Three things changed to make that
true rather than intended:

- `templates/skills/build/SKILL.md` carried a `**Sources.**` section in its
  body, which rendered into every scaffolded company. The section is removed and
  its content now lives in `templates/skills/build/SOURCES.md`, beside the skill.
- The `sources:` frontmatter key is compile-time metadata about the template and
  used to survive into the rendered document. It is now dropped at render
  (`packages/engine/src/templates.ts`), so the six adapted skills still name
  their upstreams in the file we maintain and name nothing in the file a company
  receives.
- Two entries above record verbatim upstream wording as adapted material: the
  verify section's phrase "skip any step = lying", and the plan section's
  quotation of the writing-plans audience rule. Both phrases have been rewritten
  in original prose and no longer appear in any shipped template body. Those
  entries remain accurate about what was once adapted; they no longer describe
  the current text.

A sensor now enforces the boundary instead of a convention:
`packages/engine/src/licensing_scan.ts` scans what the generators render and
ERRORS on license names, copyright lines, SPDX identifiers, attribution idioms,
and upstream repository names. It is pinned in
`packages/engine/test/templates_test.ts`.

The upstreams themselves stay MIT-named here. Nothing in this correction
withdraws an attribution; it moves each one to the file that owes it.

## packages/cli/src/runners.ts#codex-review — the independent review verb (added 2026-09-12)

Adapted from gstack (Garry Tan), MIT License — the review section of its
`codex` skill (`codex/sections/review-mode.md`). Three verified facts about
the OpenAI Codex CLI were adapted, each re-verified on codex-cli 0.154.0 and
pinned over captured output in `packages/cli/test/fixtures/codex-*`:

- scope flags and the prompt argument are mutually exclusive on `codex
  review` — a prompt beside `--base` is rejected, and a prompt alone silently
  reviews the working tree — so the verb passes `--base`/`--commit` and no
  prompt;
- top-level `codex review` has no sandbox flag; the sandbox is a config
  override (`-c sandbox_mode=…`), as is the model (`-c model=…`);
- the model is probed and the version checked before any run.

No text was copied and no launcher was adapted: gstack is not a dependency of
any batch stage (intake 2g, ruled 2026-09-11). The adapter, the assignment,
the stamp grammar and the refusal are original to this repository.

